      "observed": "The reference designator U26 is assigned to two different parts on two different sheets: on the I/O sheet U26 is a PCA9536D I2C GPIO expander (pins SDA0, SCL0, 5V_CAN_ENABLE, 3.3V_5V_PI_CONTROL, SER_SELECT, EXTRA_GPIO), while on the USB sheet U26 is a USBLC6-2P6 USB ESD/TVS diode array (pins USBAP_D_N/P -> 5V_FUSED). The two symbols carry the same ref but different part numbers and footprints.",
      "expected": "Each physical component must have a unique reference designator (standard KiCad ERC / BOM / assembly rule). The spec's component table and known-issues list document no such collision, so the intended design clearly used distinct refs for the GPIO expander and the USB ESD array.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "I/O sheet (page 2): symbol labelled 'U26' / 'PCA9536D'"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "USB sheet (page 3): ESD symbol labelled 'U26' / 'USBLC6-2P6'"}
      ],
      "impact": "A duplicated designator produces an ERC error and a BOM/pick-and-place collision: two unrelated footprints are both tagged U26, so sourcing/assembly can place the wrong part (e.g. a PCA9536 in a USBLC6 footprint or vice-versa), and the netlist/BOM cannot unambiguously identify either device. It does not change wire-level connectivity but is a real design/assembly error not covered by the documented known issues.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "All spec-cited values check out on the drawing (Y1/Y2 are 16MHz, U6 is LTC4412, U5 is ISL54222AIUZ, U8/U9 are MCP2515, D9/D10 are the CAN TVS arrays, U15/U22/U35/U36 are the e-stop buffers), and the open items (USB ideal-diode, GLOBAL_EN sequencing, CAN-LED polarity) are documented known issues, not anomalies. The one deviation the drawing supports is a duplicated reference designator U26, used for both the PCA9536D GPIO expander (I/O sheet) and a USBLC6-2P6 USB ESD array (USB sheet), which is a BOM/assembly error."
}

